Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?=F0=9F=90=9B=20Keep=20the=20OS=20cursor=20still?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20while=20the=20game=20window=20is=20in=20the=20background.?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- On Windows GLFW reports a window shown by a background process as focused
  (GetActiveWindow is per thread), so glfwSetCursorPos and GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED
  moved and confined the user's real cursor, and Minecraft grabbed the mouse
  on world join, screen close and in-world clicks.
- New OsInputGuard reads the real foreground window (JNA User32, bundled with
  Minecraft) and gates setCursorPos, the java.awt.Robot fallbacks and the
  exit_control_mode grab on it; its client-tick hook hands a background window
  focus=false through the game's own GLFW focus callback, and focus=true once
  the window is in front.
- exit_control_mode also matches the Mojang name grabMouse; the Robot
  setCursorPos fallback uses glfwGetWindowPos (glfwGetWindowPosX/Y do not exist).
- Unchanged when the window is in front, on other platforms, or when the
  foreground window cannot be read.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
